{"id":"email.headerregistry/AddressHeader","module":"email.headerregistry","qualname":"AddressHeader","name":"AddressHeader","kind":"class","parent":null,"signatures":[],"doc":"Address headers are one of the most complex structured header types. The AddressHeader class provides a generic interface to any address header.","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/email.headerregistry.html#email.headerregistry.AddressHeader","added":null,"changed":[],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":{"path":"Lib/email/headerregistry.py","line":325},"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
{"id":"email.headerregistry/AddressHeader.addresses","module":"email.headerregistry","qualname":"AddressHeader.addresses","name":"addresses","kind":"attribute","parent":"email.headerregistry/AddressHeader","signatures":[],"doc":"A tuple of Address objects encoding all of the individual addresses from the header value. If the header value contains any groups, the individual addresses from the group are included in the list at the point where the group occurs in the value (that is, the list of addresses is “flattened” into a one dimensional list).","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/email.headerregistry.html#email.headerregistry.AddressHeader.addresses","added":null,"changed":[],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":{"path":"Lib/email/headerregistry.py","line":372},"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
{"id":"email.headerregistry/AddressHeader.groups","module":"email.headerregistry","qualname":"AddressHeader.groups","name":"groups","kind":"attribute","parent":"email.headerregistry/AddressHeader","signatures":[],"doc":"A tuple of Group objects encoding the addresses and groups found in the header value. Addresses that are not part of a group are represented in this list as single-address Groups whose display_name is None.","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/email.headerregistry.html#email.headerregistry.AddressHeader.groups","added":null,"changed":[],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":{"path":"Lib/email/headerregistry.py","line":368},"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
{"id":"email.headerregistry/BaseHeader","module":"email.headerregistry","qualname":"BaseHeader","name":"BaseHeader","kind":"class","parent":null,"signatures":["BaseHeader(name, value)"],"doc":"Base class for message headers.\n\nImplements generic behavior and provides tools for subclasses.\n\nA subclass must define a classmethod named 'parse' that takes an unfolded\nvalue string and a dictionary as its arguments.  The dictionary will\ncontain one key, 'defects', initialized to an empty list.  After the call\nthe dictionary must contain two additional keys: parse_tree, set to the\nparse tree obtained from parsing the header, and 'decoded', set to the\nstring value of the idealized representation of the data from the value.\n(That is, encoded words are decoded, and values that have canonical\nrepresentations are so represented.)\n\nThe defects key is intended to collect parsing defects, which the message\nparser will subsequently dispose of as appropriate.  The parser should not,\ninsofar as practical, raise any errors.  Defects should be added to the\nlist instead.  The standard header parsers register defects for RFC\ncompliance issues, for obsolete RFC syntax, and for unrecoverable parsing\nerrors.\n\nThe parse method may add additional keys to the dictionary.  In this case\nthe subclass must define an 'init' method, which will be passed the\ndictionary as its keyword arguments.  The method should use (usually by\nsetting them as the value of similarly named attributes) and remove all the\nextra keys added by its parse method, and then use super to call its parent\nclass with the remaining arguments and keywords.\n\nThe subclass should also make sure that a 'max_count' attribute is defined\nthat is either None or 1. XXX: need to better define this API.","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/email.headerregistry.html#email.headerregistry.BaseHeader","added":null,"changed":[],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":{"path":"Lib/email/headerregistry.py","line":154},"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
{"id":"email.headerregistry/BaseHeader.defects","module":"email.headerregistry","qualname":"BaseHeader.defects","name":"defects","kind":"attribute","parent":"email.headerregistry/BaseHeader","signatures":[],"doc":"A tuple of HeaderDefect instances reporting any RFC compliance problems found during parsing. The email package tries to be complete about detecting compliance issues. See the errors module for a discussion of the types of defects that may be reported.","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/email.headerregistry.html#email.headerregistry.BaseHeader.defects","added":null,"changed":[],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":{"path":"Lib/email/headerregistry.py","line":207},"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
{"id":"email.headerregistry/BaseHeader.fold","module":"email.headerregistry","qualname":"BaseHeader.fold","name":"fold","kind":"method","parent":"email.headerregistry/BaseHeader","signatures":["BaseHeader.fold(*, policy)"],"doc":"Fold header according to policy.\n\nThe parsed representation of the header is folded according to\nRFC5322 rules, as modified by the policy.  If the parse tree\ncontains surrogateescaped bytes, the bytes are CTE encoded using\nthe charset 'unknown-8bit\".\n\nAny non-ASCII characters in the parse tree are CTE encoded using\ncharset utf-8. XXX: make this a policy setting.\n\nThe returned value is an ASCII-only string possibly containing linesep\ncharacters, and ending with a linesep character.  The string includes\nthe header name and the ': ' separator.","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/email.headerregistry.html#email.headerregistry.BaseHeader.fold","added":null,"changed":[],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":{"path":"Lib/email/headerregistry.py","line":225},"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
{"id":"email.headerregistry/BaseHeader.max_count","module":"email.headerregistry","qualname":"BaseHeader.max_count","name":"max_count","kind":"attribute","parent":"email.headerregistry/BaseHeader","signatures":[],"doc":"The maximum number of headers of this type that can have the same name. A value of None means unlimited. The BaseHeader value for this attribute is None; it is expected that specialized header classes will override this value as needed.","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/email.headerregistry.html#email.headerregistry.BaseHeader.max_count","added":null,"changed":[],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":null,"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
{"id":"email.headerregistry/BaseHeader.name","module":"email.headerregistry","qualname":"BaseHeader.name","name":"name","kind":"attribute","parent":"email.headerregistry/BaseHeader","signatures":[],"doc":"The name of the header (the portion of the field before the ‘:’). This is exactly the value passed in the header_factory call for name; that is, case is preserved.","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/email.headerregistry.html#email.headerregistry.BaseHeader.name","added":null,"changed":[],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":{"path":"Lib/email/headerregistry.py","line":203},"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
